module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.SiteCoherenceKernels
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.IdealOfGlobalSections
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.ModuleTypesOnSite
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# An ideal sheaf generated by finitely many global sections is of finite type

Let `(𝒞, 𝒪)` be a ringed site and let `f_1, …, f_r` be global sections of `𝒪`. The sheaf of
ideals `ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` they generate is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.idealOfGlobalSections` of `IdealOfGlobalSections.lean`. This file
proves that it is **of finite type** in the sense of [Stacks, Tag 03DL] part (5), both in the
sectionwise spelling `PresheafOfModules.IsFiniteType` and in Mathlib's categorical spelling
`SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`.

That is the hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 099F], so what this file records is that the case of
[Stacks, Tag 099F] which the library proves —
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_eq_idealOfGlobalSections` of
`FiniteTypeGlobalSections.lean`, for an ideal sheaf generated by finitely many *global*
sections — really does lie inside the tag's hypothesis class, rather than merely next to it.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.generates_idealOfGlobalSectionsGenerator`: over **every** object
  `U` of the site, the restrictions `f_1|_U, …, f_r|_U` generate `ℐ` in the sectionwise sense of
  `PresheafOfModules.Generates`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.presheafIsFiniteType_idealOfGlobalSections`: hence `ℐ` is of
  finite type in the sectionwise sense, with the family of *all* objects of the site for its
  covering family.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isFiniteType_idealOfGlobalSections`: **`ℐ` is of finite type in
  Mathlib's sense**, `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`.

## Implementation notes

**No epimorphism/local-surjectivity bridge is used.** Mathlib's `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`
asks for an epimorphism `𝒪^{⊕ n} ⟶ ℐ` from a *sheafified* free sheaf of modules on each member
of a covering family, and recognising such a map as an epimorphism is the comparison of
epimorphisms of sheaves of modules with locally surjective maps. That comparison is available
over a site — it is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.EpiMono.epi_iff_isLocallySurjective` of
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/Category/ModuleCat/Sheaf/EpiLocallySurjective.lean`, stated for an
arbitrary `R : Sheaf J RingCat` — but it is not needed here, because the passage from the
sectionwise condition to Mathlib's is already packaged as
`SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_of_presheaf_isFiniteType` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Topologies/ModuleTypesOnSite.lean`. So the whole proof is the
sheafification description of `ℐ` and nothing else.

**Why the covering family is the whole site.** `PresheafOfModules.IsFiniteType` asks for *some*
family of objects covering the site on each member of which finitely many sections generate.
The generators here are the restrictions of *global* sections, so they generate over every
object at once, and the family of all objects of the site — whose sieve of objects is the
maximal sieve — serves. This is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_presheaf_of_coversTop`, the
same device as `SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections.localGeneratorsData`.

**Where the sheaf condition enters.** Only through
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.mem_idealOfGlobalSections_iff`: a section of `ℐ` over `V` is one
which restricts into the ideal `Ideal.span {f_i|_Y}` along every arrow of some covering sieve of
`V`. `PresheafOfModules.Generates` asks for exactly that, with the membership in the span spelled
out as an explicit finite linear combination; the translation between the two spellings is
`Submodule.mem_span_range_iff_exists_fun`, the index type `Fin r` being finite.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 03DL](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03DL)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099B](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099B)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099F](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099F)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite

variable (X : RingedSite.{u, u, u}) {r : ℕ}
  (f : Fin r → Presheaf.globalSections (X.O.obj ⋙ forget CommRingCat.{u}))

/-- **A section of `𝒪` in the ideal generated by the restrictions `f_i|_V` is an explicit finite
linear combination of them.** This is `Submodule.mem_span_range_iff_exists_fun` at the finite
index type `Fin r`, stated at the spelling of the ambient ring in which
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.mem_idealOfGlobalSections_iff` delivers its membership, so that
neither the ring nor the module has to be inferred from the surrounding goal. -/
theorem exists_fun_of_mem_span_restrictGlobalSections (V : X.Cᵒᵖ)
    {x : ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.O)).obj.obj V}
    (hx : x ∈ Ideal.span (Set.range (restrictGlobalSections X f V))) :
    ∃ c : Fin r → ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf X.O)).obj.obj V,
      ∑ i, c i • restrictGlobalSections X f V i = x :=
  (Submodule.mem_span_range_iff_exists_fun _).1 hx

/-- The restriction `f_i|_U` of a generator, read as a section of the sheaf of ideals
`ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` over `U` rather than of `𝒪`. -/
def idealOfGlobalSectionsGenerator (U : X.C) (i : Fin r) :
    (idealOfGlobalSections X f).toSheafOfModules.val.obj (op U) :=
  ⟨globalSectionVal (f i) (op U),
    globalSectionVal_mem_idealOfGlobalSections X f i (op U)⟩

/-- **The restrictions of the generators generate `ℐ` sectionwise over every object.** For every
object `U` of the site, the finite family `f_1|_U, …, f_r|_U` of sections of
`ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` over `U` generates `ℐ` in the sense of `PresheafOfModules.Generates`: every
section of `ℐ` over an object `V` above `U` restricts, along every arrow of some covering sieve
of `V`, to an `𝒪`-linear combination of the restrictions of the family.

By `Stacks.SheafOfModules.generates_submodule` the statement may be read in the ambient `𝒪`
rather than in the submodule, where it is exactly
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.mem_idealOfGlobalSections_iff` — the sheafification description of
`ℐ` — with the membership in `Ideal.span {f_i|_Y}` turned into an explicit combination. Note
that no arrow `V ⟶ U` is used: the generators are restrictions of global sections, so the
covering sieve produced does not depend on `U` at all. -/
@[ega "0 15.2.1" "more general"]
theorem generates_idealOfGlobalSectionsGenerator (U : X.C) :
    PresheafOfModules.Generates X.J (idealOfGlobalSections X f).toSheafOfModules.val
      (idealOfGlobalSectionsGenerator X f U) := by
  refine Stacks.SheafOfModules.generates_submodule _ _ fun V g t => ?_
  obtain ⟨S, hS, hSmem⟩ := (mem_idealOfGlobalSections_iff X f (op V)).1 t.2
  refine ⟨S, hS, fun W k hk => ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := exists_fun_of_mem_span_restrictGlobalSections X f (op W) (hSmem k hk)
  exact ⟨c, Eq.trans (Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ =>
    congrArg (fun y => c i • y) (globalSectionVal_map (f i) (k ≫ g).op)) hc⟩

/-- **The ideal sheaf generated by finitely many global sections is of finite type in the
sectionwise sense**, [Stacks, Tag 03DL] part (5) as `PresheafOfModules.IsFiniteType` spells it.
The covering family may be taken to be the family of *all* objects of the site, since by
`generates_idealOfGlobalSectionsGenerator` the generators generate over each one. -/
theorem presheafIsFiniteType_idealOfGlobalSections :
    PresheafOfModules.IsFiniteType X.J (idealOfGlobalSections X f).toSheafOfModules.val :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_presheaf_of_coversTop (id : X.C → X.C)
    (fun _ => X.J.covering_of_eq_top
      (Sieve.ext fun Y _ => ⟨fun _ => trivial, fun _ => ⟨Y, ⟨𝟙 Y⟩⟩⟩))
    fun U => ⟨r, idealOfGlobalSectionsGenerator X f U,
      generates_idealOfGlobalSectionsGenerator X f U⟩

/-- **An ideal sheaf generated by finitely many global sections is of finite type**, in Mathlib's
sense `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType` — which is [Stacks, Tag 03DL] part (5).

This is the hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 099F]. So the proved case of that tag,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_eq_idealOfGlobalSections` of
`FiniteTypeGlobalSections.lean`, is a case *of the tag* and not merely an analogue of it: the
tag's hypothesis holds of every ideal sheaf that statement applies to. What the tag has and the
statement has not is the ideal sheaf which is only *locally* generated.

The passage from the sectionwise condition to Mathlib's categorical one is
`SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_of_presheaf_isFiniteType`; its hypotheses are the sheafification
instances on the slice sites that `SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections` needs there, and none of
them is a finiteness condition. -/
theorem isFiniteType_idealOfGlobalSections
    [∀ U : X.C, HasWeakSheafify (X.J.over U) AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
    [∀ U : X.C, (X.J.over U).WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}] :
    (idealOfGlobalSections X f).toSheafOfModules.IsFiniteType :=
  SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_of_presheaf_isFiniteType _
    (presheafIsFiniteType_idealOfGlobalSections X f)

end AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite

end
